details.image_prompt:
[
  "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The scene is composed at a 3/4 overhead angle, ensuring the entirety of two matte ceramic plates is visible. The primary plate is sharp in the foreground, with a second, identical plate slightly blurred in the background. Each serving features a generous portion of the Moroccan-style braise: succulent, bite-sized chicken tenderloin chunks, perfectly opaque and tender, are nestled among fluffy grains of cooked quinoa and firm, golden-toned chickpeas. All ingredients are coated in a rich, vibrant tomato-based sauce that is deeply colored by the warm hues of cumin, smoked paprika, and cinnamon. Fresh baby spinach is wilted throughout the mixture, adding splashes of deep, dark green. Soft natural light from a window on the left highlights the glistening sheen of the olive oil and lemon juice within the sauce. The plates sit on a weathered, aged oak wood table with visible grain and natural use marks. Beside the front plate lies a wooden spoon resting casually, along with a few stray chickpeas and a small, unpeeled garlic clove on the wood surface. The depth of field is shallow, creating a soft, creamy bokeh. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene should look like someone just finished cooking and plating — lived-in, warm, authentic. Not a sterile studio setup. Slight natural imperfections: an asymmetric garnish placement, a small sauce drip on the plate rim, slightly uneven browning on the chicken. Include subtle traces of the cooking process: a crumb trail on the surface, a small spinach leaf on the table, a slight oil mark near the plate. No plastic sheen, no unnaturally smooth surfaces, no perfect symmetry. Warm, golden-hour color temperature — cozy and inviting, not cold or clinical. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. The image should be indistinguishable from a photograph taken by a professional food photographer for a printed cookbook."
]
